  "query_preview": "..\n\nNotable inversion on the 00Z DTX RAOB last evening has low level \nmoisture trapped across southeast Michigan. Low stratus has been \nslow to clear along and east of the glacial ridge, but the back edge \nof this cloud shield is slowly creeping east through the early \nmorning hours. To the west of this cloud deck is clear skies and \nfavorable environment for fog development with many observations now \nshowing visibility down to 1/2 mile or less. Will monitor the \nvisibility trends that could necessitate a dense fog advisory. \n\nAny fog across southeast Michigan is expected to dissipate by 15Z \nthis morning. Brief residence time of shortwave ridge today with a \nsurface high pressure to the south will strengthen southwest low \nlevel flow and commence strong warm air advection into this \nafternoon. Temperatures at 850 mb are forecast to be around 15C this \nafternoon and good insolation under mostly clear skies will allow \nhigh temperatures to reach the upper 70s to low 80s. Strengthening \nLLJ winds will drive increasing moist isentropic ascent over \nMichigan tonight as a weak low pressure moves along a cold frontal \nboundary draped across the upper Midwest. This will lead to the \ndevelopment of showers that will move across a good portion of \nsoutheast Michigan during the overnight hours. There will be just \nenough elevated instability to support isolated general \nthunderstorms.\n\nSurface low forecast to track across northern lower Michigan on \nTuesday. Southwest to west flow will draw in slightly warmer air \nwhile allowing the mild airmass to persist throughout the day. \nExpecting 850 mb temperatures to climb a 2-3 more degrees compared \nto today with mid-upper level drying bringing a period of reduced \nclouds. This should help temperatures a little higher into the low \n80s for much of the area with mid 80s achievable towards the \nsouthern MI border ahead of the approaching front. The cold front is \nexpected to pass through late Tuesday and offer a low chance for \nadditional shower activity, though better chances will reside to the \nnorth. \n\nAnother chance for precipitation will occur Tuesday night into \nWednesday as a shortwave is forecast to lift across the lingering \nfrontal zone. Activity will be more focused south of I-69 corridor of\nsoutheast Michigan into the Wednesday afternoon period and come to \nan end by Wednesday evening. Wednesday will also begin the transition\nback to more typical fall like conditions that carry into the late \nweek. Higher amplitude ridging will build across the Intermountain \nWest during this time with a downstream strengthening Canadian trough\ncombining with a southern stream wave. Another round of deeper \nmoisture with ties to the gulf will lead to widespread precipitation \nwith periods of steadier rainfall supported by the greater dynamic \nsetup moving over the Great Lakes. In the wake of this system will \nbe strong cold air advection that will result in a cool start to the \nweekend. Saturday morning lows will fall towards freezing with \ndaytime high temperatures topping out in the low-mid 50s. It does \nappear though that temperatures will recover back towards more normal\nvalues Sunday into early next week.",
